## <div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shopping at the Market – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 4th Grade (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shopping at the Market</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Need Five Yucas.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: reading1: Write short responses to questions about a text.
reading2: Outline key information in simple factual texts on familiar subjects.
phonemicAwareness: Blend spoken simple onsets and rhymes to form real words (onset /c/ and rhyme /at/).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can write short responses to questions about items and prices (e.g., 'How many apples?' 'Five apples, please.').
Can read and outline key information about prices and quantities in texts about the market or groceries (e.g., 'Four bananas cost one dollar.').
Can blend simple spoken onsets and rhymes to form familiar words (e.g., /t/ and /ɛn/ to form the word 'ten').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
        <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher shows flashcards or realia of market items (pineapple, cassava, potatoes, apples, bananas) and numbers 1-10. Students identify the items and practice numbers. The teacher asks, "What do you see?" and students respond, "I see a pineapple," "I see five apples." The teacher then introduces the theme "I Need Five Yucas." by showing a picture of yucas and saying, "I need five yucas!"</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up flashcards and clearly pronounces the names of market items and quantities, emphasizing the target sounds /p/ in 'pineapple' and /m/ in 'money'. The teacher models key phrases: "I need five potatoes," "How much is the pineapple?" and "It costs one dollar." The teacher also models blending simple onsets and rhymes for numbers, for example, saying "/t/ /ɛn/" and then blending it to "ten," or "/f/ /aɪv/" for "five," asking students to repeat the blended word.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of key vocabulary and concepts.</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is 'pineapple' a fruit or a vegetable?" (Expected answer: "A fruit.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say '/w/ /ʌn/', what number is it?" (Expected answer: "One.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do we say 'How much' for big things or for prices?" (Expected answer: "For prices.")</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, simple market stall sign or a handwritten shopping list with items and prices. For example:</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-family: 'Comic Sans MS', cursive;"><b>Fresh Market!</b><br>
                Pineapples: $3 each<br>
                Yucas: $1 for two<br>
                Potatoes: $2 for three<br>
                Bananas: $1 for four<br>
                Apples: $1 for two</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will read a simple factual text to understand information about items and their prices at a market.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher asks, "What is this paper about?" (Expected answer: "It's about prices" or "It's about the market.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Activity:</b> Students read sentences and say if they are true or false based on the market sign.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One pineapple costs three dollars." (True)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Three yucas cost one dollar." (False - two yucas cost one dollar)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Five bananas cost one dollar." (False - four bananas cost one dollar)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">8-10 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading and identifying specific information using WH-Questions ("How much?", "How many?"), Quantities, and Present Simple verbs (cost, need, want). Vocabulary includes market items, price, cost, numbers 1-100.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Cloze Reading):</b> The teacher provides a worksheet with sentences that have blanks, based on the market sign from Stage 2. Students read the sentences and fill in the missing words (numbers or item names).
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One pineapple costs ______ dollars." (three)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"You can buy ______ yucas for one dollar." (two)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Three potatoes cost ______ dollars." (two)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Four ______ cost one dollar." (bananas)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Reading (Question-Answer Drill):</b> The teacher writes simple WH-questions on the board or provides them on a worksheet. Students read the questions and write short answers based on the market sign.
                    <ul>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How much is one pineapple?" (It is three dollars.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How many yucas cost one dollar?" (Two yucas cost one dollar.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How much are three potatoes?" (They are two dollars.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Check & Read Aloud:</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed worksheets from activity 2. They read their partner's answers and check them against the original market sign. They politely correct any errors by pointing to the text. For example, "Look, on the sign, four bananas cost one dollar, not three."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Shopping at the Market".</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Reading and Shopping List Creation.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher provides each group with a slightly modified or expanded "Market Special" flyer (text) with different items and prices (e.g., adding 'mangoes', 'oranges', 'fresh cheese'). The theme "I Need Five Yucas." is reinforced.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Each group receives a budget (e.g., "You have $10 to spend!").</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read the market flyer together.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They discuss what they "need" or "want" to buy for their families, ensuring they stay within budget. (e.g., "I need two pineapples. That's six dollars." "But I want five yucas, that's two dollars. Can we buy both?")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They collaboratively create a shopping list by writing down the items and quantities they choose, and the total cost for each item. Example:
                    <p style="text-align: left; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-family: 'Comic Sans MS', cursive;"><b>My Shopping List:</b><br>
                        2 Pineapples = $6<br>
                        5 Yucas = $2.50<br>
                        Total = $8.50</p>
                </li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group reads their shopping list aloud to the class, stating what they "need" or "want" and "how much it costs."</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Reading comprehension for specific details (prices, quantities), outlining key information from a text, and expressing needs/wants using target grammar and vocabulary in a communicative context.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during group work and a short individual written response activity.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students receive a new, very short market advertisement or flyer (2-3 items with prices). They read the flyer and then answer two or three written questions about it individually on an exit ticket. This directly assesses their ability to "write short responses to questions about a text" and "outline key information".</p>
            <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0; font-family: 'Comic Sans MS', cursive;"><b>Fresh Fruits!</b><br>
                Oranges: $1 for 5<br>
                Mangoes: $2 each</p>
            <p style="text-align: left; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">1. How many oranges cost one dollar?</p>
            <p style="text-align: left; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2. How much is one mango?</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of information in written responses.</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct use of numbers and target vocabulary (e.g., 'oranges', 'mangoes', 'dollar').</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity of written answers (legible and understandable).</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Participation and collaboration during the group activity (observed).</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts:</p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today, I learned to read about _______ and _______ at the market."</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can find the _______ of an _______."</li>
            </ul>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>

    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was easy for me when reading about prices and items?"</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part was a little difficult, and how can I practice it more?"</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this reading lesson? (e.g., "Students were engaged with the market sign and the shopping list activity allowed them to apply their reading.")</li>
                <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson? (e.g., "Some students needed more time to read the full sentences; I need to provide more visual aids for quantities. I should also explicitly review number blending at the start of the next lesson.")</li>
            </ul>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> This lesson focused on reading about market items and prices. In Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), students will use the knowledge gained from reading to verbally ask about prices and describe quantities in a role-play scenario, actively speaking about "Shopping at the Market" and expressing their needs like "I need five yucas."</p>
        </td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">2 min</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>